A||||6||mshill||Anyone with an EGR tube that's not broken?||||5
Z||000000||mshill||10-08-1999||02:23 AM||||I have been from bone yard to bone yard and have yet to find a V6 EGR tube that is not broken.  I need one, but refuce to pay $90 for the braided stainless aftermarket one that is offered.  Of course, the only place to get one is from the dealer.  Anyone know what dealers are charging for thes pieces of garbage?<P><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 10-08-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||10-08-1999||08:48 AM||||The Fiero Store also carries EGR tubes, but your still going to be playing around $90.  I believe its a little cheaper at the dealer, but I wouldn't bet your going to get it much cheaper.  There is a GM dealer I believe out west who sells stuff to the "Fiero Community" at cost, rather than with the regular dealer markup.  People on the "other list"(fiero.org) have spoken of this magical place but I have never payed much attention, as I am not buying anything at the time. I'm sure there are others out there that know what I am speaking of.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||10-08-1999||12:37 PM||||Most of the time, it is possible to repair the old tube by brazing it.  You could just trim up a piece of coke can and use it to seal off the EGR tube at the intake plenum until you locate another.  I have heard of  using a tube from a 2.8 firebird (or equiv.)  The bolts on the one flange are spaced a bit differently, but with a dremel you can make the bolt holes into slots which allow the tube to bolt up to the Fiero.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||JIM GOOSE||10-08-1999||02:32 PM||||I had a broken tube also & blocked off the openings on both manifolds ,removing the EGR valve entirely. Vacuum line to the valve was plugged.  I did buy a tube (80$) later after deciding it would be near impossible to fix the old one. Presently, have a BB in the vacline to the valve so its not operating but appears stock from outside.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ezsurf||10-09-1999||12:40 AM||||I also had a split egr tube, but I found something to fix it.  I used some stuff called thermo steel.  It's good to 2400 degrees F.  Found it at Wal-Mart for about $5.00.  So far, it's holding up great.  I orginally tried JB Weld, but it wouldn't take that much heat.  I wasn't going to pay $90 either.  I checked at a dealer, a new one was $75.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||10-09-1999||01:18 AM||||Thanks for the info.  I just put JB Weld on mine (about 3 thick coats) and put it back in the car this afternoon.  Next time it pops I will get the thermo steel stuff.  I am getting real fast at taking it out and putting it back in.<P>||||reg||11
Z||000006||FieroGT||10-11-1999||01:56 PM||||Hey, Guys! I have a cheap fix for this one. Just visit your local junkyard and get the EGR tube from any Camaro or Firebird (86-92) with a V6. I got 2 EGR tubes for $5!!! A little modification has to be made, though. The plenum side will bolt up fine, but the side that goes to the EGR has to have its' flange holes slotted to bolt up to the EGR. Took a while to get the slots done, because the tube flange is hard! Probably should have taken it to the machine shop and saved the aggravation. I didn't have to pull the plenum to do this, but if I had to do it again, the plenum would definitely come off, because it makes the job much easier. <P>The tube is a bit lager in diameter, and does not have the protective silver heat blanket around it. If these little details don't bother you, than this may be your fix. ||||reg||10
